-- title:
--   One machine step costs a fixed number of recorder steps
-- statement:
--   A checkpoint of a recorder machine $M$ is a Turing machine configuration with a history: a state, an infinite left part of the tape, the scanned symbol, a finite right part, and a history stack. Its expansion is the recorder configuration in the ready state whose right stack is the scanned symbol, the right part, a boundary marker and then blanks. The next checkpoint performs one transition of $M$ and records it on the history. If the state of a checkpoint $c$ is not the halting state, then the recorder runs from the expansion of $c$ to the expansion of its next checkpoint in exactly
--   $$
--   2\bigl(|r|+1\bigr)+2
--   $$
--   steps, where $|r|$ is the length of the finite right part of $c$.
--
--   So the recorder simulates each Turing machine step by a sweep to the boundary and back, followed by one execution step.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/OAI/MathematicalPhysics/NavierStokes/BalancedTransport/RecorderSimulation.lean#L184-L205

